--- AN HONEST NOTE ON HOW (a) MANIFESTS, AND WHY (b) IS CAPTURED SEPARATELY ---

The RED of proof (a) turned out to be SHARPER than the "no oneOf branch
matches" wording anticipated, and this is stated plainly rather than glossed.
`tests/evidence/test-helpers.ts:78` constructs ajv as
`new Ajv2020({ strict: true })`, and ajv's `strict: true` DOES enable
`strictRequired`. So the schema does not merely fail to validate — it fails to
COMPILE:

  Error: strict mode: required property "undeterminable" is not defined at
  "https://sdd-forge.dev/contracts/sdd-forge-mcp-tools.v1.schema.json#"
  (strictRequired)

`getEnvelopeValidator()` therefore THROWS, and every one of the 70 failing
tests below fails with that identical error — including all 4 of T-004's new
runtime cases, whose first statement inside `callFindMissing` is the
`getEnvelopeValidator()` call.

The consequence must be named: proof (a) MASKS proof (b). In this `npm test`
run the 4 new runtime cases are red because the ajv validator cannot compile,
NOT because they observed a missing `undeterminable` field. Their
field-level expectations were never reached. Presenting this single run as
evidence that "TEST-007's assertion failed against the absent field" would be
an overclaim, so it is not presented that way.

Proof (b) is therefore captured independently, in the third command below: a
scratchpad script (NOT a tracked file; the working tree is not modified to
produce it) imports the already-compiled `evidenceFindMissing` and
`parseTaskState` from dist-test/ and calls them against the same
scan-failure fixture shape TEST-007 uses. It records, at runtime and without
ajv in the path, that:
  * `Object.keys(result.data)` is
    ["kind","feature","taskId","required","present","missing"] --
    `"undeterminable" in result.data` is FALSE;
  * `quality-gate-report-pass` is in `missing` for a directory-scan FAILURE,
    which is precisely the conflation issue #132 reports;
  * `parseTaskState` reports `done-quality-gate-report-missing` for the same
    task -- the right-hand side of the parity biconditional.
That is the genuine, unmasked assertion-level RED.

